.TH GH 1 "2026-08-16" "realisateur" "bashified verb"
.SH NAME
gh \- sign every GitHub comment this account writes
.SH SYNOPSIS
.\" bashify: norun -- every form reaches the real gh, which writes to GitHub
.B gh
[gh arguments...]
.br
.B gh
--self-check
.br
.B gh
--stamp
.br
.\" bashify: norun -- takes a path or reads stdin
.B gh
--check-body <path>
.br
.B gh
--help
.br
.\" bashify: norun -- answered by the real gh
.B gh
--version
.SH DESCRIPTION
A shim installed ahead of the real
.B gh
on PATH. Everything it does not recognise passes through untouched, so any
invocation documented by GitHub CLI still works and still means the same thing.
.PP
It does two things of its own. It appends
.B <!-- agent: <account>@<host> <ISO8601> <build> -->
to the bodies of
.BR "issue comment" ,
.BR "issue create" ,
.BR "issue close" ,
.B "pr comment"
and
.BR "pr create" ,
so those writes are attributable with no argument a caller can forget. And it
refuses an
.B issue create
or
.B pr create
whose body breaks the grammar in bin/lib/body-grammar.sh, before the body
exists, while it is still free to fix.
.PP
The trailing field of the stamp names the copy of the policy that wrote it.
Past fourteen days that build is stale, every body it signs carries
.BR "STALE <n>d" ,
and the refresh is demanded on stderr at each write. It still writes: an
unsigned comment is the status quo, a dropped one is not.
.PP
There is exactly one copy. It is edited as bin/gh-sign.sh on realisateur's
main branch, carried here by bin/carry-drift.sh, and linked into
/usr/local/bin by the release tick. Editing the installed file is the one
thing never to do, and bin/carry-drift.sh reports it.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B --self-check
Print which real gh it resolves to, the stamp it writes, which build this copy
came from and how old it is, and which grammar library it grades with.
.TP
.B --stamp
Print the stamp this host and account append.
.TP
.B --check-body
Grade a body against the grammar without writing anything.
.B -
reads standard input.
.TP
.B --help
This page's summary, printed only where no real gh exists to answer it.
.TP
.B --version
Answered by the real gh.
.SH EXAMPLES
An empty body names every rule it breaks, and says so with an exit code:
.PP
.nf
$ gh --check-body /dev/null
UNDECLARED  no first line to declare on.
UNLEDGERED  no <!-- DEFERRED --> block. Say what was left behind, or "- none".
$ echo $?
3
.fi
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.TP
.B 0
the real gh's own exit, or a --self-check that found nothing wrong.
.TP
.B 1
the real gh's own exit, or --self-check on a build past the age limit.
.TP
.B 3
a body was refused by the grammar. Nothing was created.
.TP
.B 6
BLIND: no real gh to run, or no grammar library to grade with.
.TP
.B 127
a write was asked for on a host with no gh at all.
.SH COST
Costs nothing to run and reaches no paid service. It cannot spend money, and
has no --summon.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR git (1)
.SH NOTES
mandark is deliberately unshimmed: an unsigned comment from a human's own
machine is the signal decision-rot reads.
